From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: stubbyfowl <sidaksmann@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 21:23:36 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] fix: use raw strings for regex literals

Eight regular expressions were written as plain string literals
containing backslash sequences that are not valid string escapes
(\. \d \D \s \/). Python has kept the backslash for these since forever,
but 3.12 started emitting SyntaxWarning for them at compile time
("invalid escape sequence") and the language plans to make them a
SyntaxError in a future release.

Prefixing each with r makes the intent explicit. None of the eight
contains a sequence that is a valid string escape (\n, \t, \b, ...), so
every pattern is byte-for-byte identical before and after; this was
checked by evaluating each old and new literal and comparing them.
python -W error::SyntaxWarning -m compileall nasp/ scripts/ now passes.

  nasp/configuration_parser.py:73, 116
  nasp/dispatcher.py:50, 75, 103, 228
  nasp/nasp.py:71, 102

Refs: NASP bioconda diagnosis (FINDINGS.md), Attempt 07, Category 3
item 6.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 nasp/configuration_parser.py | 4 ++--
 nasp/dispatcher.py           | 8 ++++----
 nasp/nasp.py                 | 4 ++--
 3 files changed, 8 insertions(+), 8 deletions(-)

diff --git a/nasp/configuration_parser.py b/nasp/configuration_parser.py
index 166962c..e7fe308 100755
--- a/nasp/configuration_parser.py
+++ b/nasp/configuration_parser.py
@@ -70,7 +70,7 @@ def _find_reads(folder, filepath):
 
     num_reads = 0
     for file in os.listdir(filepath):
-        is_read = re.search('(.*)(\.fastq(?:\.gz)?)$', file, re.IGNORECASE)
+        is_read = re.search(r'(.*)(\.fastq(?:\.gz)?)$', file, re.IGNORECASE)
         if is_read:
             sample_name = is_read.group(1)
             is_paired = re.search('^(.*)(_[R]?)([12])(.*)$', sample_name, re.IGNORECASE)
@@ -113,7 +113,7 @@ def _find_files(folder, filepath, filetype, extension):
 
     num_files = 0
     for file in os.listdir(filepath):
-        is_type = re.search('(.*)(\.%s)$' % extension, file, re.IGNORECASE)
+        is_type = re.search(r'(.*)(\.%s)$' % extension, file, re.IGNORECASE)
         if is_type:
             sample_name = is_type.group(1)
             file_node = ElementTree.SubElement(folder, filetype, {'sample': sample_name})
